 "key_big": "n² = n × n, n³ = n × n × n",
 "key_sub": "√ undoes squaring; ∛ undoes cubing.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>The square of 13 is 13² = 169. The cube of 4 is 4³ = 4 × 4 × 4 = 64.</li><li>√169 = 13 and ∛64 = 4.</li><li>To find a square root by prime factors, put the factors in pairs and take one from each pair: 324 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 3 × 3 × 3, so √324 = 2 × 3 × 3 = 18.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>√576</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>576 = 2⁶ × 3² → 2³ × 3</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 24</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>∛125</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>5 × 5 × 5 = 125</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 5</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Writing 4³ = 12</td><td>4³ = 4 × 4 × 4 = 64.</td></tr><tr><td>Dividing by 2 for a square root</td><td>√64 = 8, not 32.</td></tr><tr><td>Dividing by 3 for a cube root</td><td>∛27 = 3, not 9.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
